"dev_how": "How it is protected, and its limits",
"dev_how_1": "Each device has its own secret key. Pairing with the one-time code proves both devices are yours, and each then remembers the other's key.",
"dev_how_2": "Everything between your devices travels encrypted with TLS 1.3, the same standard that protects banking websites. Your internet provider and anyone in between see only scrambled data.",
"dev_how_3": "There is no Datas server in the middle and no account. A device that was not paired is refused.",
"dev_how_4": "On the same Wi-Fi or cable network it works directly. Between different places you need an address that reaches the other device: a private network such as WireGuard or Tailscale, or a port opened on the router.",
"dev_how_5": "Joining memory lets a bigger AI fit; it does not make answers faster, because every word passes through all the devices.",
"dev_how_6": "If a device drops out in the middle of a chat, Datas continues on this device with the best AI that fits here. For now linking works between computers; the phone app cannot link yet.",
